## 2. Work queue — do in this order

### P0 — before the first class

- [ ] **Instructor sequence sign-off:** read FE I and FE II as tracks, not isolated lessons. Confirm that every assessed deliverable has an earlier practice opportunity and a named individual-evidence channel.
- [ ] **Freeze scope:** record the package/runtime versions used for 2026-27 demonstrations. Separate version-dependent HOW from the durable master idea.
- [ ] **Run the technical smoke matrix below** on a clean student account or lab machine.
- [x] **Freeze assessment instruments:** add a version and frozen date to [`docs/evaluation/shared/ai-declaration-oral-defence-rubric/index.md`](../web-foundations/docs/evaluation/shared/ai-declaration-oral-defence-rubric/index.md). Keep the grading version stable for the cohort.
- [ ] **Campus Virtual delivery data:** enter actual dates, groups, submission links, deadlines, recovery procedure, and communication channels in the institutional system.
- [ ] **Accessibility route:** verify keyboard, focus, captions/transcripts, reduced motion, colour/contrast, non-WebGL fallback, offline failure messaging, and accessible live status.
- [ ] **First-session rehearsal:** time Unit 1, verify the AI-declaration explanation, and confirm that its 0 h lab allocation is communicated rather than accidentally filled.
- [ ] **Release checkpoint:** commit/deploy the selected version and inspect `/tracks/fei/`, `/tracks/feii/`, and at least Units 2, 8, and 10 on the public URL.

#### Technical smoke matrix

| Unit | Required rehearsal before delivery | Pass condition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2–3 Astro | Fresh install, build, SSG/SSR path, one hydrated island, content/data boundary | Student can run the example and explain what crosses the boundary |
| 4 PWA | Installability, first load, offline reload, stale cache, update, reconnect | Failure and recovery states are visible; “installable” is not mistaken for “offline” |
| 5 Testing | Test commands, deterministic fixture, CI timing, contract mock, accessibility floor | Commands run as written; no timeout-based flake masking |
| 6 AI review | PR permissions, prompt inputs, accept/reject/escalate log, secret-safe workflow | No private code/token leak; human decision remains assessable |
| 7 Performance | Repeatable measurement, bundle/asset budget, before/after record | Student reports observed evidence, not a single magic score |
| 8 R3F | Scene, camera, interaction, render loop, `renderer.info`, non-WebGL fallback | Scene works on lab hardware and student can explain the render model |
| 9 Shaders | GLSL compile, uniforms, one authored change, failure diagnostics | Generated appearance without explanation does not pass |
| 10 IoT/Python | Mock sensor, WebSocket open/reconnect/close, stale state, safe command, FastAPI shape | Works without physical hardware; unsafe/stale action is blocked and announced |
| 11 Integration | Decision/iteration logs and AI declaration integrated into a sample repository | Required evidence is inspectable from the repository |
| 12 Defence | Timed mock defence and diff-based corrective question | Rubric can be applied consistently by two reviewers |

**P0 definition of done:** every required command and interaction has been run on the delivery environment; students know what to submit, when, how it is assessed, and what fallback exists.

